{"reference":"Judges 17","book":"Judges","chapter":17,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Household Worship and Comparison","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Micah’s house contains an image, an ephod, teraphim, and a priest, and he expects the Levite’s presence to bring him prosperity. The chapter therefore portrays worship organized at the household level and tied to hoped-for benefit. A small Iron I open cult place known as the Bull Site offers a broad comparison for religious activity outside a monumental urban temple, but it cannot securely be linked with Micah, Dan, Shiloh, or any event in Judges.","verse_refs":["Judges 17:1-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_017_7f03f78d2e6f46f45511f2a1"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"“No King in Israel”","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Verse 6 is part of a repeated closing refrain connecting the absence of a king with people doing what seemed right in their own eyes. It raises a question about Israel’s lack of governing order, but the refrain alone does not settle the larger question of what forms of kingship the book endorses.","verse_refs":["Judges 17:6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_017_37e62a48b02f9ec9699e2417"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Judges in Its Larger Movement","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Judges moves from incomplete possession and covenant indictment through recurring deliverance stories toward internal idolatry, atrocity, and civil war. Micah’s household shrine belongs to this later movement, where the book’s attention turns increasingly to disorder within Israel itself.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_017_83cef256eb5473ba1d89a9b1"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}